PART 3 -- what the unread Table III could actually cost.
====================================================================================================
If T0 were really 300 K rather than the 1 K these entries carry, A would be wrong by
300^n. Per entry that factor is:

  entry 14  n =   0.01   300^n =    1.059x
  entry 15  n =   0.36   300^n =    7.794x
  entry 16  n =  -0.02   300^n =    0.892x
  entry 21  n =   0.41   300^n =   10.366x
  entry 22  n =   0.14   300^n =    2.222x
  entry 23  n =    0.0   300^n =    1.000x

But every comparison this branch DECIDED on is Ozawa-against-Ozawa, so a convention
error hits both sides and the ratio moves only by 300^(n1-n2):

split                                as decided  worst other        vs 4x  verdict
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
N2_neutral (O2p + N vs O2p + N2)         106.8x        48.1x 12.0x margin  SURVIVES
O2_neutral (NOp + O vs NOp + O2)         217.7x        22.2x  5.6x margin  SURVIVES

So the T0 convention, even if it is wrong, cannot overturn either split: the worst
case still clears the criterion. The unread Table III is a gap in the RECORD, not a
risk to a decision this branch already made.

